  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(211.26pt, 83.22pt, "Solar cells and thermoelectric generators ")
  #line-at(224.34pt, 97.02pt, "as finite-time chemical heat engines ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(274.204pt, 120.423pt, "Tom Markvart* ")
  #line-at(165.907pt, 131.946pt, "School of Engineering Sciences, University of Southampton, UK, and ")
  #line-at(157.03pt, 143.469pt, "Centre for Advanced Photovoltaics, Czech Technical University in Prague")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.02pt, 189.441pt, "ABSTRACT")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(126.499pt, 189.441pt, " This paper shows a fundamental thermodynamic similarity between thermoelectric and photovoltaic ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 200.964pt, "energy converters which, at open circuit, can be represented as isochoric engines generating a finite chemical potential ")
  #line-at(72.03pt, 212.427pt, "which appears as voltage at the terminals of the device. We show that, allowing for the temperature variation of the ")
  #line-at(72.03pt, 223.95pt, "Seebeck coefficient, the maximum energy efficiency is intrinsically lower than the Carnot efficiency, as assumed in ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 235.473pt, "most of the literature, although more sophisticated strategies may exist to recuperate this loss. At finite current, further ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 246.936pt, "losses can be modelled in terms of finite-time thermodynamics. ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 281.444pt, "Thermodynamic theories of thermoelectric generators ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 292.967pt, "continue to attract the interest of the research ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 304.49pt, "community (see, for example, [1-6]).  Observed for the ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 315.953pt, "first time more than two hundred years ago [7,8], the ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 327.476pt, "thermoelectric phenomena were explained in terms of ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 338.999pt, "classical thermodynamics [9], later refined with the ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 350.462pt, "understanding of irreversible thermodynamics [10, ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 361.985pt, "11]. The first observation of the photovoltaic effect ")
  #line-at(72.02pt, 373.508pt, "dates to a similar time as the discovery of ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 384.971pt, "thermoelectricity [12] but practical applications of ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 396.494pt, "solar cells and thermoelectric  generators  had to await ")
  #line-at(72.01pt, 408.017pt, "the advent of semiconductors in the mid 20")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(245.1pt, 404.46pt, "th")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(250.14pt, 407.94pt, " century.  ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 430.926pt, "In this note we demonstrate a fundamental similarity  ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 442.389pt, "between ")
  #line-at(117.428pt, 442.389pt, "the ")
  #line-at(141.733pt, 442.389pt, "thermoelectric ")
  #line-at(211.576pt, 442.389pt, "and ")
  #line-at(238.036pt, 442.389pt, "photovoltaic ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 453.912pt, "generators pointing out, at the same time, inherent ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 465.435pt, "irreversibilities and losses in the conversion process. ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 476.898pt, "We show that, at open circuit, both types of converters  ")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 488.421pt, "work as isochoric chemical engines, where electrons ")
  #line-at(71.994pt, 499.944pt, "or photons as the working medium generate a finite ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 511.407pt, "chemical potential equal to the output voltage. ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 522.93pt, "Extraction of current from the generator and the ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 534.453pt, "resulting losses are naturally modelled in the language ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 545.916pt, "of finite-time thermodynamics. ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 568.961pt, "To this end, we consider the thermoelectric generator ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 580.424pt, "at open circuit, as an energy (rather than power) ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 591.947pt, "converter, transforming heat into the electrostatic ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 603.47pt, "energy ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(103.367pt, 603.47pt, "qVoc")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(123.948pt, 603.47pt, " of charge carriers at the contacts. The ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 614.933pt, "generator legs are formed by n and p type semicon-")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 626.456pt, "ductors, with sufficient density of doping and high ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 637.979pt, "enough bandgap to limit the conductivity to a single ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 649.442pt, "type of charge carrier in each leg (Fig.1). The ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 660.965pt, "conversion process is described by Kelvin’s relations ")
  #line-at(71.984pt, 672.488pt, "[13] ")
